# Problem Solving and Reasoning. S = questions in slide order, R = connection questions.
S = [
 ([2], "What is a problem?", "A problem involves an obstacle between a present state and a goal, with a solution that is not immediately obvious."),
 ([2], "What are the present state and the goal?", "The present state describes the situation as it currently exists, and the goal describes the situation the person is trying to reach. The obstacle prevents an immediate move from one to the other."),
 ([2], "Why does knowing the goal not solve the problem, as in the numbered-tile puzzle?", "A person can identify exactly what counts as a solution and still have a problem, because the sequence of moves that produces it is unclear."),
 ([3], "What is the Gestalt approach to problem solving?", "Gestalt psychologists took a perceptual approach to learning, problem solving and attitudes and beliefs as well as perception, and Koffka (1935) is associated with this approach. Problem solving involves representing a problem in the mind."),
 ([3], "What is restructuring?", "Restructuring means reorganizing the problem’s representation, so a previously hidden relationship becomes usable."),
 ([3], "How is Köhler’s circle problem solved?", "The black line is one diagonal of a rectangle whose lower-right corner is at the circle’s center, and the dashed line from the upper-left corner on the circle to the center is a radius. The diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so x = r."),
 ([4], "What is insight?", "Insight is the sudden realization of a problem’s solution after perceiving an object or problem one way and then representing it differently."),
 ([4], "What is the Aha experience?", "Gestalt psychologists connected insight with the Aha experience, in which the person goes suddenly from not having the solution to recognizing it, instead of feeling a series of increasingly complete steps."),
 ([4], "Is every problem solved with an Aha experience?", "No. Some problems permit a more methodical process in which successive operations bring the person closer to an answer."),
 ([5], "What did Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) compare?", "They compared insight problems, including the triangle and chain problems, with noninsight problems, including algebra, and had participants judge how close they felt to a solution."),
 ([5], "What were Metcalfe and Wiebe’s (1987) predictions for insight and noninsight problems?", "For insight problems, people would not be very good at anticipating how near they were to a solution. For noninsight problems, successive operations would give information about progress, so perceived closeness should rise as the solution approaches."),
 ([5], "How were warmth judgments collected in Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)?", "Participants made warmth judgments every 15 seconds, with hot meaning close to the solution and colder meaning farther away."),
 ([6], "How is the triangle problem solved?", "Ten dots form an upward triangle and exactly three must move to make it point down. The top dot becomes a new bottom point beneath the center of the bottom row, and the two outer dots of the bottom row move to the ends of the two-dot row."),
 ([6], "How is the chain problem solved?", "Opening a link costs 2 cents, closing costs 3 and the budget is 15, so three opened-and-closed links are possible. One three-link section is opened into three links that join the other three sections into a closed loop."),
 ([6], "Why must seven dots stay in place in the triangle problem?", "The task is to move exactly three dots, so relocating the entire arrangement would violate it."),
 ([7], "What did the warmth ratings show for algebra and for insight problems in Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)?", "In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), ratings for algebra increased gradually as the solution approached. For insight problems, warmth stayed low until close to the solution and then jumped sharply, from about 3 to 7."),
 ([7], "What do the warmth results in Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) suggest about insight?", "In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), a low rating shortly before an insight solution means the person did not yet feel close even though the answer was imminent, so insight differs from methodical noninsight solving."),
 ([8], "What is fixation?", "Fixation is a tendency to focus on a specific characteristic of a problem in a way that prevents a solution, and Gestalt psychologists treated it as a major obstacle."),
 ([8], "What is functional fixedness?", "Functional fixedness restricts an object to its familiar functions."),
 ([8], "How is the candle problem solved?", "The matchbox is attached to the corkboard with a tack and used as a platform for the candle, so the candle can burn without dripping wax on the floor."),
 ([8], "What happened when participants were given empty matchboxes in the candle problem?", "They were twice as likely to solve the problem, because separating the box from its familiar contents made it easier to treat it as an object with another use."),
 ([9], "How is the two-string problem solved?", "A pair of pliers is tied to one string and used as a weight. Setting that string swinging makes a pendulum, so the person can hold the other string and catch the weighted one."),
 ([9], "How does functional fixedness appear in the two-string problem?", "The familiar tool function of the pliers prevents their use as a weight, since the useful property is weight and not the ability to grip."),
 ([10], "What happened when the experimenter accidentally set a string in motion?", "Most participants were able to solve the problem, because the moving string made its use as a pendulum available."),
 ([10], "What is a mental set?", "A mental set is a preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar problems. It concerns the method of solving."),
 ([10], "What is the water-jug problem?", "It asks for a required amount of water using three empty jugs with specified capacities."),
 ([11], "How does the water-jug procedure B − A − 2C work?", "In the first problem A holds 21 quarts, B holds 127, C holds 3 and the goal is 100. Filling B and removing one A-sized amount leaves 106, removing one C-sized amount leaves 103, and a second leaves 100."),
 ([11], "How does a mental set hide a simpler solution in problems 7 and 8?", "In problem 7 (A 15, B 39, C 3, goal 18) the familiar method works, but A + C gives 18 directly. In problem 8 (A 28, B 59, C 3, goal 25) A − C gives 25 directly, so the set inhibits a simpler solution even though the familiar one still works."),
 ([12], "What did Newell and Simon (1956) develop, and how did they describe problem solving?", "They developed the Logic Theorist and described problem solving as a process involving search, which includes the activity between posing a problem and its solution."),
 ([12], "What is a problem space?", "A problem space includes an initial state, the intermediate states and a goal state. Knowing the initial and goal states does not specify which intermediate states connect them."),
 ([13], "What are operators?", "Operators are rules specifying which moves are allowed and which are not. Applying one changes the problem from one state to another."),
 ([13], "What are the rules of the Tower of Hanoi?", "A move transfers one disk at a time, a disk can be moved only when no other disk is on top of it, and a larger disk cannot be placed on top of a smaller disk, even in intermediate arrangements."),
 ([13], "What are the initial, goal and intermediate states in the Tower of Hanoi?", "The starting stack is the initial state, the completed stack on the target peg is the goal state, and temporary distributions across the pegs are intermediate states."),
 ([14], "Why does the Tower of Hanoi need repeated rearrangements?", "Smaller disks must be moved out of the way before a larger disk becomes available, and after the largest disk is moved the smaller disks must be rearranged onto it in order."),
 ([14], "How long would the 64-disk Tower of Hanoi take?", "Roughly a trillion years, even if every correct move took one second, because the number of required moves is very large."),
 ([15], "What are the initial and goal arrangements of the numbered-tile puzzle?", "The initial rows are 8–empty–6, 5–4–7 and 2–3–1, and the goal rows are empty–1–2, 3–4–5 and 6–7–8."),
 ([15], "Why is having all the tiles on the board not enough in the tile puzzle?", "A tile being present on the board does not mean its required position has been achieved. The current state has to be related to the goal through an allowable sequence of moves."),
 ([16], "What is a search tree?", "A search tree represents possible states of a tile puzzle and the moves connecting them. Each state can have several successors, so the solver chooses which branches to pursue."),
 ([16], "What are the legal moves from the start 2–8–3, 1–6–4, 7–empty–5?", "The empty location can shift left, up or right by exchanging position with the adjacent tile, and each result is a different intermediate state with the same eight tiles."),
 ([16], "What do the numbered labels and the small boards show in the search tree?", "The numbered labels identify positions in the search, and the small boards specify the actual tile arrangements."),
 ([17], "What is means–end analysis?", "Means–end analysis organizes the search around reducing the difference between the current situation and the goal, by considering which changes would remove an obstacle."),
 ([17], "What is a subgoal?", "A subgoal is a condition that makes part of the larger task possible, not the complete final answer. Achieving it can reduce an obstacle that prevents a required move."),
 ([18], "What are the three subgoals in the Tower of Hanoi?", "First, free the large disk by moving the smaller disks off it. Second, free the third peg by moving the smallest disk from it onto the stack on the middle peg. Third, move the large disk from the starting peg to the third peg."),
 ([18], "Why do the Tower of Hanoi subgoals address different obstacles?", "One removes the material above the disk that must move, and another removes an obstruction at its destination, so several coordinated changes are needed before the overall move becomes legal."),
 ([19], "How can the way a problem is stated change its difficulty?", "The wording and presentation influence which relationships become part of the representation, so a useful property can be easier or harder to recognize even when the underlying task is the same."),
 ([19], "What is the mutilated-checkerboard problem?", "It concerns covering a board with dominoes after squares have been removed, asking whether the covering is possible under the domino constraints."),
 ([19], "What does Newell and Simon’s approach provide for specifying a solution?", "A way to specify possible pathways from an initial state to a goal state. Identifying states and possible transitions makes the search explicit, and a solution requires an allowable route between the beginning and the required endpoint."),
 ([20], "Why can opposite removed corners not be covered by 31 dominoes?", "Removing two opposite corners leaves 62 squares, matching the area of 31 dominoes, but every domino covers one square of each color and the two removed corners are the same color. The remaining board has unequal colors, so the covering is impossible."),
 ([20], "Does equal total area guarantee that dominoes can cover the board?", "No. 62 squares matches the area of 31 dominoes, but equal area does not guarantee a legal arrangement, because every domino must cover one square of each color."),
 ([21], "What conditions varied how the board squares were described?", "Participants received a blank board, a colored board, a board labeled black and pink, or one labeled bread and butter."),
 ([21], "What did the bread-and-butter condition show?", "Participants solved it twice as fast as the blank condition, because the labels emphasized the pairing of the two kinds of square and made the constraint easier to represent."),
 ([22], "What is a think-aloud protocol?", "A person says aloud what they are thinking while solving a problem, verbalizing new thoughts instead of describing their actions."),
 ([22], "What do think-aloud reports show in the bread-and-butter checkerboard?", "Participants reach the solution after recognizing that the words bread and butter are relevant to the required pairing. Their thinking shifts from squares as positions to the relationship between two kinds of square, which is a representational shift related to restructuring."),
]
R = [
 ("How does the Gestalt approach differ from the search approach of Newell and Simon (1956)?", "The Gestalt approach emphasizes how a problem is represented and restructured, with insight as a sudden realization. Newell and Simon describe solving as searching a problem space of states using operators.", [3,4,12]),
 ("How do insight and noninsight problems differ in the experience of progress?", "In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), algebra warmth rose gradually, while insight warmth stayed low and then jumped sharply, so insight does not give a steady feeling of getting closer.", [4,5,7]),
 ("How are restructuring and insight related?", "Restructuring is the change in representation, and insight is the sudden realization that follows once a crucial element is found in the new representation.", [3,4]),
 ("How do functional fixedness and mental set differ?", "Functional fixedness restricts an object to its familiar function, while a mental set is a preconceived approach to solving based on past experience. Both are obstacles to solving, but one concerns objects and the other concerns methods.", [8,10]),
 ("How do the candle problem and the two-string problem both involve functional fixedness?", "In each, an everyday object, a matchbox or pliers, has a familiar function that blocks seeing its useful property, a platform or a weight.", [8,9]),
 ("Why does the swinging-string hint count as restructuring?", "The hanging string changes from something to be reached where it is into something that can be made to swing within reach, so the representation changes.", [9,10]),
 ("How do the empty matchboxes in the candle problem and the bread-and-butter labels in the checkerboard both show that presentation changes difficulty?", "Participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve the candle problem, and participants given bread-and-butter labels solved the checkerboard twice as fast as the blank condition. In each case the presentation made a useful relationship easier to represent.", [8,21]),
 ("How do the water-jug problems show that past success can hurt?", "Repeated success with B − A − 2C builds a mental set, so the simpler A + C or A − C solutions can be missed even though the familiar method still works.", [10,11]),
 ("How are initial, intermediate and goal states, operators and search related?", "Operators define legal moves that change one state into another, and search is finding a sequence of those moves from the initial state through intermediate states to the goal.", [12,13,16]),
 ("How do the Tower of Hanoi and the tile puzzle both illustrate a problem space?", "Each has an initial state, a goal state, intermediate states and rules for legal moves, and solving requires choosing a route through those states.", [13,15,16]),
 ("Why can a subgoal be a temporary arrangement that is not the final stack?", "A subgoal makes a later required move legal, such as freeing the large disk, even though the temporary arrangement is not the goal.", [17,18]),
 ("How does means–end analysis relate to search?", "It organizes search around reducing the difference between the current state and the goal, using subgoals.", [12,17,18]),
 ("How do problem statement and the Gestalt idea of representation connect?", "Both say that the way a problem is represented, including wording and labels, changes which relationships are noticed and therefore how hard it is.", [3,19,21]),
 ("How does the mutilated-checkerboard solution use a constraint that applies to all placements?", "The color pairing of every domino and the same-color removed corners show impossibility without trying individual placements.", [19,20]),
 ("Why did bread and butter help, and how does this relate to restructuring?", "The labels pointed toward the pairing of two kinds of square, which changed how squares were represented, matching the Gestalt idea that representation shifts make the solution available.", [3,21,22]),
 ("How does the think-aloud method let researchers see a representational shift?", "Verbal reports show when the pairing of the two labels entered the person’s reasoning, so the shift from squares as positions to a relationship can be observed during the attempt.", [22]),
 ("Why do the triangle and chain problems count as insight problems in Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)?", "They are solved by a change in representation, such as using a whole section as connecting links, and they were compared with algebra, which has a more methodical route.", [5,6]),
 ("How are the Gestalt obstacles to solving different from limits of a search?", "Fixation, functional fixedness and mental set are obstacles from how the problem is represented or approached, while the 64-disk Tower of Hanoi shows that the number of required moves can be very large.", [8,10,14]),
 ("What do all the problem types share about a present state, a goal and an obstacle?", "Each has an obstacle between a present state and a goal, and the solution is found by restructuring the representation or by searching for a route.", [2,3,12]),
 ("How do insight problems and noninsight problems differ in how a solution is reached?", "Insight is a sudden realization after restructuring, and in Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) warmth for insight problems stayed low and then jumped sharply. Noninsight problems such as algebra allow a more methodical process, and warmth rose gradually.", [4,5,7]),
]
